diff --git a/docs/storage-usage.md b/docs/storage-usage.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..5f0f472
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/storage-usage.md
@@ -0,0 +1,23 @@
+# Storage usage
+
+`Account#bytes_used` and `Board#bytes_used` read the latest persisted totals calculated by backend jobs. Changes become visible when those jobs run, without reloading the account or board. Empty and unsaved records return zero.
+
+Usage counts each card image attachment and each file embedded in a board's public description, a card description, or a comment body. Draft cards count too. A blob referenced by multiple attachments counts once per attachment. Avatars, unattached uploads, remote images, generated previews/variants, and import/export archives do not count.
+
+## Existing installations
+
+After running the migrations, enqueue the initial backfill:
+
+```sh
+bin/rails storage:reconcile
+```
+
+Run the backend queue workers to calculate the totals. The task is safe to repeat and can also reconcile attachments changed through bulk SQL, which bypasses Rails callbacks. Account imports enqueue their own backfill; derived totals are not imported from the source boards.
+
+## Updates and concurrency
+
+Attachment creation, removal (including detach and purge), card moves, and record destruction enqueue reconciliation after transaction commit. Each job reads current attachment ownership rather than trusting a potentially stale queued snapshot.
+
+A uniquely indexed ledger row per counted attachment records its last applied board and byte size. Reconciliation locks that row and applies only the difference to the account and board counters in the same database transaction. Counter updates use atomic SQL increments, with stable board lock ordering and retries for transient lock/uniqueness conflicts. Duplicate and out-of-order jobs are harmless. Removed attachments leave no ledger row.
+
+Ordinary attachment changes do constant-size work, independent of account or board size. Moves, deletions, and backfills enumerate attachment IDs in bounded batches rather than loading all blobs or recalculating an entire board on every edit. Reading usage never scans attachments or downloads files.
